    "justification": "An all-test converse makes the scale an information bound rather than a quadratic limitation.",
    "gap": "GaoHarshawSavjeWang2026, arXiv version 2, Proposition 4.1 is an MSE upper envelope supported by Lemmas S3--S4 and has no all-test converse; Tiwari--Basu study power by simulation, and Kandiros et al. target estimation.",
    "consumer": "The theorem identifies when no analysis of the same Bernoulli arm can reliably detect smaller spillovers.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(\\delta_{\\mathrm{sq}}\\) satisfying the displayed hypothesis and put \\(a=\\sqrt{\\delta_{\\mathrm{sq}}}\\).  First, this amplitude lies in the range used by the hard subexperiment.  Indeed, Jensen's inequality and \\(\\mathbb ET_a=1/2\\) give\n\\[\n\\xi_H=\\sum_{a\\in V_H}\\mathbb ET_a^4\\ge h/16,\n\\]\nand Theorem 1 and the definition of \\(L_H\\) imply\n\\[\na\\le\\sqrt\\varepsilon L_H(V_r^{\\mathrm{quad}})^{1/4}\n=\\sqrt\\varepsilon\\,(C_{\\cos}\\xi_Hr)^{-1/4}<\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus Lemma 2 applies.  Substitution of \\(L_H^4=h^2/(C_{\\cos}\\xi_Hv_H)\\) and \\(V_r^{\\mathrm{quad}}=v_H/(rh^2)\\) into its affinity bound yields\n\\[\nH^2(P_{0,a},P_{1,a})\n\\le C_{\\cos}r\\xi_Ha^4\n\\le C_{\\cos}r\\xi_H\\varepsilon^2L_H^4V_r^{\\mathrm{quad}}\n=\\varepsilon^2. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAverage the test over its auxiliary randomization and denote the resulting \\([0,1]\\)-valued function of \\((Z,Y^{\\mathrm{obs}})\\) by \\(\\bar\\phi\\).  Since the null prior in Lemma 2 is supported on \\(\\mathcal B_{0,r}(H)\\) and \\(\\phi\\in\\Psi_{\\alpha,r}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{P_{0,a}}\\bar\\phi\\le\\alpha. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nFor any two dominated laws with densities \\(p,q\\), Cauchy--Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|P-Q\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n&=\\frac12\\int|\\sqrt p-\\sqrt q|\\,(\\sqrt p+\\sqrt q)\\\\\n&\\le\\frac12H(P,Q)\\{4-H^2(P,Q)\\}^{1/2}\\le H(P,Q).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTherefore (13)--(14) imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{P_{1,a}}\\bar\\phi\n\\le\\mathbb E_{P_{0,a}}\\bar\\phi+|P_{1,a}-P_{0,a}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\le\\alpha+\\varepsilon. \\tag{15}\n\\]\nEvery schedule in the support of the alternative prior lies in \\(\\mathcal B_{1,r}(H,a^2)=\\mathcal B_{1,r}(H,\\delta_{\\mathrm{sq}})\\).  The left side of (15) is the prior average of the fixed-schedule powers.  At least one schedule in that support must consequently have power at most \\(\\alpha+\\varepsilon\\), proving the claimed infimum bound.  The displayed formula for \\(L_H\\) uses only \\(v_H\\) and \\(\\xi_H\\), both computed from the stated motif table and program.",
